    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `clean_orders` in `app/clean.py`. I ran it on a small sample of orders and all three rules came out right, and the existing test passes (`pytest -q`: 1 passed). That test only checks that the function exists, so the checks below are the real evidence.\n\nWhat the function does:\n- **Text columns:** trims leading and trailing spaces, and anything left empty (including values that were only spaces) becomes a missing value. With your pinned pandas 3.0, text read from the CSV gets pandas' dedicated string type, so the function handles that type and older-style mixed \"object\" columns. In mixed columns only actual strings are touched; numbers and other values stay as they are.\n- **`qty`:** missing values become 0. Because the column had gaps, it stays a decimal type (e.g. `1.0`, `0.0`) rather than switching to whole numbers.\n- **Everything else**, including `price`, is unchanged. It works on a copy, and I confirmed the input frame is left unmodified.\n\nIn the sample, `\" A1 \"` became `\"A1\"`, a note of just spaces became missing, a blank `qty` became `0.0`, and a blank `price` stayed missing.\n\nI couldn't delete the scratch script I used for testing because the delete command needed your approval, so please remove `scratch_probe.py` from the project root yourself. No dependencies were added."
